timeline.body:
@chiragramani do you have large tree artifacts on the inputs or outputs of that action?

My guess is that this is caused by the naive way parallel checksumming just starts as many threads as there are cores, without much respect for anything else. Thus, if there is already high load (which is totally possible, given that it's the middle of a build), badness happens.

The band-aid here is to add a flag to control whether parallel checksumming should happen, but the principled solution would be to take a good hard look at how Bazel managed CPU-bound work. We should do that in any case because it would help in other scenarios, too, but maybe this is the bug that would provide the impetus to do so?

@meisterT WDYT?
